<a id="_Toc182825516"></a>__Abstract__

To store and manage a variety of digital materials, including visuals, sounds, scripts, and 3D models, often, independent developers use standard file storage services. However, as the complexity of the project grows, this approach leads to challenges related to organization of assets, difficulties in retrieval, inconsistency in metadata and weak collaboration management. On top of that, tagging of assets by hand takes a lot of time and efforts and still ends up with incomplete and inaccurate metadata thus hindering discovery of an asset and its efficient workflow.

The goal of this project is to create an AI\-based collaborative digital asset management platform especially designed for independent game developers. This platform should provide an all\-in\-one place where digital materials necessary for game development can be stored, organized, retrieved and controlled with the help of Role\-Based Access Control (RBAC). An AI\-assisted metadata generation pipeline aimed at automatically generating tags for different types of assets is the key feature of the system that would help with better organization of assets and help to minimize the efforts put into tagging assets by hand.

The RAD, or Rapid Application Development, methodology was applied in the project, which prioritizes feedback and adjustment during design. The architecture of the system was created using a web framework featuring React.Js, Node.Js with Express.Js, PostgreSQL, MinIO, Redis, FastAPI, and Docker, and several AI technologies, including CLIP, KeyBERT, YAMNet, and Whisper. The system functions and interactions with users were illustrated by developing UML diagrams, database architectures, and prototype designs.

The resulting prototype shows primary capabilities such as user authentication, workspace administration, asset upload and sorting, AI\-enabled tagging, advanced asset search, versioning, collaboration via comments, and administration. This platform provides a groundwork for improving how assets are discovered and metadata consistency in independent game production environments.

