## <a id="_Toc237556007"></a>Digital Asset Management (DAM) in Game Development

Digital asset management (DAM) is fundamentally concerned with aligning digital content with organizational objectives and operational needs, prioritizing the intended use and contextual value of assets rather than focusing solely on their technical storage or preservation (Currall & Moss, 2011). As organizations continue to produce increasing volumes of multimedia content, traditional file management approaches become insufficient for maintaining efficient asset organization. Consequently, DAM has evolved into a comprehensive management approach that combines structured organization, metadata, and lifecycle management to improve asset accessibility and operational efficiency.

Generally, modern DAM systems comprise a number of critical elements, including centralized storage, the issue of metadata management, searching functions, control of versions, facilitating collaboration, storage management, and management of access. These elements operate in tandem to heighten the efficiency of storing, getting organized, retrieving, and safeguarding digital means throughout their lifecycle. Metadata management is especially important because it makes assets findable and supports searchability across the system (Xing, 2021). Thus, it seems reasonable to conclude that effective DAM system should be perceived as a unified management system rather than just a solution for storage.

Effective DAM systems require centralized storage to maintain digital assets efficiently, particularly when large multimedia files are involved. As the repository continues to grow, storage scalability becomes increasingly important to ensure long\-term system performance (Liao & Abadi, 2023). In addition to centralized storage, metadata plays an important role in improving asset discoverability. Proper metadata allows assets to be categorized and searched efficiently, reducing the reliance on manual browsing and inconsistent file naming (Gendron et al., 2021).

In the context of game development, this alignment becomes much more complicated when considering the variety and interdependence of the types of assets, involved in this process. In contrast to many conventional business settings where the only digital resource is documents, game development involves a variety of multimedia resources that are subject to numerous modifications during the production process. The game project uses several types of digital assets, such as 2D sprites, 3D models, animation, audio files, texts and configuration data that perform their own functions in the production process.

The limitations of conventional DAM demonstrate that modern asset management systems require more than centralized storage and structured organization. As collaborative development becomes increasingly common, technologies that improve security, metadata quality, and workflow automation have become essential. Therefore, Role\-Based Access Control (RBAC) and AI\-assisted auto\-tagging are reviewed in the following sections as important technologies supporting the proposed system.

However, despite the availability of existing DAM systems, many solutions still rely on manual processes for organizing and tagging assets, which are time\-consuming and lead to inefficient asset management workflows (Sandoval\-Castañeda et al., 2024). This suggests that conventional DAM approaches may no longer adequately support the complexity of modern game production, particularly as asset collections continue to expand.

Effective game\-oriented DAM therefore requires not only reliable storage, but also meaningful metadata, efficient retrieval mechanisms, and collaboration support that reflects how assets are created, shared, and iterated in practice. This need is particularly pronounced in independent game development, where small teams must manage growing asset collections with limited production resources and frequent role overlap, as team members often assume multiple creative and managerial responsibilities simultaneously (Rodriguez Castañeda, 2025).

## <a id="_Toc237556008"></a>Role\-Based Access Control (RBAC)

Role\-Based Access Control (RBAC) is an access control model in which permissions to system resources are assigned based on user roles rather than individual user identities, allowing organizations to manage access rights more efficiently by grouping users according to their responsibilities. Instead of granting permissions directly to each user, RBAC associates access rights with predefined roles that represent specific functions within a system, and users obtain the required permissions by being assigned to these roles (Ferraiolo et al., 2007). This approach simplifies access management, especially in environments with multiple users, as it reduces complexity and helps ensure that permissions are consistently applied across the system.

In collaborative digital systems, RBAC provides a structured method for controlling access by linking permissions to organizational roles instead of individual users, which makes it easier to manage access in multi\-user environments. By clearly defining roles and their associated permissions, RBAC helps prevent unauthorized access and reduces the risk of assigning excessive or incorrect permissions. This not only improves system security but also enhances operational efficiency, as administrators can manage user access more easily and accurately. (Male, 2025; Owoade et al., 2023).

Such structured permission management is especially relevant in collaborative game development environments, where multiple team members interact with shared digital assets. Role\-based permission structures help ensure that access to resources is aligned with each user's responsibilities while supporting teamwork. In addition, RBAC enhances system security by preventing unauthorized access and reducing the risk of unintended modifications, thereby protecting asset integrity in collaborative environments.

## <a id="_Toc237556009"></a>AI\-assisted Auto tagging

AI\-assisted metadata management refers to the use of artificial intelligence techniques to support the automatic generation and organization of metadata for digital assets, reducing the reliance on manual input and improving overall efficiency in asset management processes. As digital asset collections continue to expand in both scale and diversity, traditional manual tagging practices become increasingly difficult to maintain, as they require significant time and effort from users while often leading to inconsistent results across different contributors. In asset\-intensive environments, manual tagging is commonly prone to errors, omissions, and variations in classification, which negatively affect asset discoverability and reuse  (Angerri et al., 2025; Oyighan et al., 2024).

To address these limitations, AI‑assisted metadata management has emerged as a practical enhancement to traditional asset organization approaches. By leveraging automated tagging and metadata enrichment techniques, AI systems can support the generation of descriptive and semantic metadata at scale, reducing the dependency on manual intervention while improving consistency and organizational structure. Rather than replacing human decision‑making, such approaches are commonly positioned as assistive mechanisms that augment existing metadata workflows and help maintain manageable asset repositories (Saraiya, 2025).

In collaborative production environments, particularly in independent game development, AI\-assisted metadata and auto\-tagging are highly relevant due to the continuous creation, modification, and sharing of digital assets among team members with different roles and responsibilities. Automated tagging reduces the cognitive and administrative burden associated with manual classification, making it easier for developers to manage large collections of assets efficiently. At the same time, allowing users to review, modify, or override AI\-generated tags ensures that specialized or creative assets are not incorrectly classified, maintaining both accuracy and flexibility in asset organization.

