Redis is widely utilized as a message queue and as an in\-memory data storage application to conduct asynchronous processing. By virtue of being a messaging layer, Redis comes into play in the case of the system when it is used as a queue to store tasks associated with the AI processing. The moment digital assets are uploaded by the users, the backend system sends tagging requests to Redis, which means that the tag processing takes place without any interruption in the user experience because the job run is performed asynchronously. In the domains where heavy computing tasks are involved, such as AI processing, Redis allows separating the execution process of the system from the operation of the main application server providing asynchronous computing.

### <a id="_Toc237556023"></a>Docker

By using containerization technologies like Docker, a homogeneous environment is created for implementing applications by means of packing together software parts with their dependencies. In the suggested platform, Docker serves to run frontend application, backend server, PostgreSQL database, MinIO object storage, Redis message queue, and AI classification system as independent containers. Docker makes it possible to implement uniformly in production and development environments, making system configuration easier and supporting multi\-service architectures with the help of such tools as Docker Compose. As a result of isolating each service from others, as well as ensuring their interaction, deployment becomes more reliable, maintenance is easier, and scalability is improved.

## <a id="_Toc237556024"></a>Database

Database technology is essential for digital asset management systems as it permits effective storage, organization and retrieval of data and metadata. Relational databases are largely used in these systems because they are capable of maintaining structured data, ensuring data reliability (Pallante et al., 2024). Similarly, they facilitate large\-scale data organization and querying, making them suitable for dealing with complicated asset management issues.

![](source_converted_files/image_015.png)

Since its introduction, PostgreSQL has gained significant popularity in recent years due to its various features and capabilities. PostgreSQL utilizes a stable table\-based framework allowing metadata to be kept in clearly defined schemas which guarantees consistency and reliability of the system. Lastly, it offers support for various sophisticated indexing forms and query optimization methods ensuring the efficient retrieval of huge data flows.

One of the main reasons why PostgreSQL is popular among businesses is its ability to work with both structured and semi\-structured data. Support for JSON and JSONB data types is key to adapting to complex metadata that typically exists in digital asset systems. Also, with support for transactions, PostgreSQL is able to provide reliable data storage. The technology comes with scalable features that can adapt as the volume of data grows. 

Due to these features, PostgreSQL is appropriate for asset management systems with the need for efficient metadata storage and offers a flexible way of organizing data. Nevertheless, relational databases are likely to have problems with non\-structured multimedia, showing the necessity of using other methods of storage.

## <a id="_Toc237556025"></a>Summary

The focus of this chapter has been to analyze the most important notions and technologies of digital asset management applied in game development. The starting point in this regard is based on the work of Currall and Moss (2011), who underlined that Digital Asset Management (DAM) stands for much more than simple storage, because it involves meaningful organization and utilization of digital assets. Based on this idea, Rodriguez Castañeda (2025) pointed out the growing difficulties of independent game developers working with collections of assets that are both heterogeneous and growing quickly.

In order to create productive collaborative environments, Ferraiolo et al. (2007) introduced Role\-Based Access Control (RBAC), while Owoade et al. (2023) point out its importance in this field of access control in connection with the question of providing secure collaboration. The studies cited evidence the role of access control in the safeguarding of assets in multiuser systems.

Due to the drawbacks of manual asset management, research by Oyighan et al. (2024) and Angerri et al. (2025) focused on AI\-enabled metadata management to enable automated tagging and thus achieve both efficiency and consistency. Nevertheless, Saraiya (2025) revealed that many implementations of currently available systems make AI tagging an independent characteristic rather than integrating it into the asset management process.

The deficiency in current systems is illustrated by some of them, like LottieFiles which specializes in animated assets by supporting minimal types of game assets; Google Drive that mainly relies on manual organization of files with limited capabilities of structured metadata and advanced access solutions. As was shown in the comparison analysis, none of the studied systems are capable of delivering an integrated solution that brings together automation, cooperation, and management of structured assets.

To fill the voids noticed in the previous sections, the advanced systems architecture made use of several new technologies to provide it with better performance abilities and increased scalability. Technologies like React.js and Node.js are used for implementing dynamic web\-based applications. Therefore, MinIO is used for efficient storage of unstructured data, Redis is for performing asynchronous processing, and Docker is chosen for achieving flexible deployment environments. PostgreSQL has emerged as an effective relational database used to handle structured metadata and guarantee data integrity.

To sum up, the literature highlights the fact that although advancements in digital asset management have been made, no integrated solution exists that ensures intelligent automation, management of structured metadata, and trustworthy collaborative control at the same time. Based on this observation, the current research intends to work out an AI\-based collaborative platform for digital asset management which is going to be described in details below.

