## <a id="_Toc237556051"></a>Class Diagram

<a id="_Toc236834072"></a><a id="_Toc236860109"></a>Figure 4.15 Class Diagram of the Proposed System

![](source_converted_files/image_037.png)

Figure 4.15 illustrates the class diagram of the proposed AI‑Enhanced Collaborative Digital Asset Management Platform. This diagram describes the important classes of the design with their attributes, methods, and relationships. The major areas covered by the design are user management, workspace collaboration, digital asset management, AI\-assisted metadata creation, version management, and collaboration. The design uses a modular approach to ensure ease of maintenance and scalability. All classes are divided into three modules: User and Workspace Management; Asset and AI Metadata Management; and Version Management and Collaboration.

The User and Workspace Management module handles authentication, user\-related permissions, and workspace management. The User class acts as a primary entity responsible for holding account\-related data, authentication procedures, and control over account status. Users can create more than one workspace and keep active sessions through a UserSession class.

The workspace class describes a collaborative setting where digital resources are organized and shared between users. The membership of a workspace is controlled via the WorkspaceMember class through which roles and rights are assigned according to a role\-based access control policy. The Invitation class provides users with the ability to use invitation codes to access workspaces.

The Asset and AI Metadata Management module is in charge of the organization and management of stored digital assets. The Asset entity acts as the core concept and keeps track of the basic information of assets such as name, type, description, category, and project link. All assets belong to a workspace and can be managed in the system.

The AssetMetadata entity stores structured metadata related to each asset while the AssetTag entity enables the user to manage tags that can be created either automatically or manually, making the asset more searchable. The class of the physical asset is represented by StoredFile, which keeps track of the storage details including the location, the total size of the files, and their checksum.

In order to facilitate efficient asset management, all activities involving AI processing are represented using the AIProcessingJob class. This class is used for monitoring all AI\-related activities, such as the processes of metadata extraction and generating tags, performed by the multi\-modal pipeline of AI.

The Version and Collaboration module offers the ability to track versions and engage in discussions with other members of the workspace. The AssetVersion class makes it possible to keep track of the history of uploaded versions which makes it possible to download old pictures and record necessary changes that need to be made during the creation process.

The Comment class allows collaborating through associating comments with the pictures being discussed. There is an opportunity to comment, respond to existing comments, edit the comment made and manage threads. The recursive nature of the relationship created by the Comment class allows for maintaining the connection in case of nesting the responses, thus providing the possibility of organized discussions related to the pictures kept in the repository.

The class diagram shows how the new system connects all the elements of user management, workspace collaboration, smart asset management, AI\-enabled metadata development, version control, and communications. The relationships between the classes stretch the structure of the system, help maintain data accuracy, provide role\-based access, and ensure that the structure of the system allows implementation of all functionalities described in the use case and sequence diagrams.

