# <a id="_Toc237556073"></a>Conclusion

## <a id="_Toc237556074"></a>Introduction

This chapter concludes the work completed during the Final Year Project 1 (FYP 1) phase. It summarizes the achievements accomplished throughout the project, discusses the limitations encountered during the current development stage, and outlines the future work planned for the next phase of the project. The chapter also provides an overall summary of the proposed AI‑Enhanced Collaborative Digital Asset Management Platform and its contribution to addressing asset management challenges in independent game development.

## <a id="_Toc237556075"></a>Achievements

During the FYP 1 phase, notable accomplishments were achieved in the area of creating and developing an AI\-augmented Digital Asset Management system that could be utilized by independent game developers. The project made a significant step towards the framework and technical development of the designed digital asset management tool.

One of the essential achievements of the project was the strengthening of knowledge in the area of Digital Asset Management (DAM) and its application in the context of collaborative game development. The literature review and analysis of other systems such as Google Drive, LottieFiles, and Eagle contributed to gaining important information about the factors related to asset organization, metadata management, collaboration, and access control. This allowed stakeholders to identify the shortcomings of current solutions and to substantiate the necessity of developing a more specialized platform.

Moreover, information on Role\-Based Access Control (RBAC) and metadata management was obtained for the study. This work gave insight into structured permission models for enhanced collaborative security and the means of using artificial intelligence for automation of metadata creation and classification. Several AI technologies, ranging from CLIP to YAMNet, were studied in terms of their applicability for management of multimedia assets.

The project also enriched technical knowledge concerning web application systems' architecture and software development techniques. The system architecture was built utilizing React.js, Node.js with Express.js, PostgreSQL, MinIO, Redis, FastAPI, and Docker. As a result of this work, hands\-on experience was received in frontend development, backend services, object storage, database operations, and artificial intelligence applications.

Futhermore, the project afforded the team important experience with software engineering practices and system modelling techniques. The team drew up various types of UML diagrams such as use case diagrams and sequence diagrams that facilitated modelling system functionality and user interactions. The team also put in place database structures, user workflows, and system architecture so that the implementation phase could be supported. These endeavours deepened the understanding of system analysis, requirement engineering as well as programming methodologies.

Another significant achievement involves creating a working prototype that illustrates the concept's main functions. The prototype comprises user authentication capabilities, workspace administration, asset management, AI\-assisted labelling, advanced searching, version control, collaboration properties, and administration options. It clearly lays out what the complete system will look like and serves as a base for implementation and evaluation during the FYP 2 phase.

In general, the accomplishments acquired during the FYP 1 phase have enabled obtaining theoretical understanding and practical skills in the field of digital asset management, integrating artificial intelligence, designing collaborative systems, and developing contemporary web applications. These results create a solid foundation for the following implementation, testing, and evaluation activities at the next phase of the project.

## <a id="_Toc237556076"></a>Limitations

Currently, the scope of this initiative is completely restricted to the analysis of the systems, designing systems, and creating prototypes. So far as the architecture of the system, database design, workflows for the users, and interface prototypes are concerned, these have been worked out. However, the system and all its components have not yet been implemented. The pending items include the AI\-based tagging pipeline, object storage solution, asynchronous processing methods, as well as the environment of deployment.

The subsequent point is that the efficiency of the functionality associated with AI tagging has not been verified on real datasets. Although the appropriate AI models, such as CLIP, KeyBERT, YAMNet, and Whisper, have been found and included in the system architecture design, their accuracy and quality of the metadata created have not been verified by experiments showing real results. Therefore, it is not possible to evaluate the efficiency of the method of the AI\-assisted metadata generation.

Moreover, no evaluations have been conducted for usability and user acceptance at this time. As the system is still in proof\-of\-concept, no independent game developers have been able to provide any feedback about the usability, user satisfaction, and work process efficiency of the system. Hence, the proposed platform has not yet been quantitatively tested for the extent to which it improves asset management.

In addition, also performance\-related matters such as system scalability, response time, storage efficiency, and the ability to support multiple concurrent users are waiting for testing too. These evaluations need a fully working system and a wide testing environment, which would be provided in the next stage of the project.

Despite these limitations, the work done during the FYP 1 phase has enabled the establishment of conceptual, architectural, and functional basis needed for the project in phases FYP 2.

## <a id="_Toc237556077"></a>Future Work

The next step in FYP 2 will involve implementing the system architecture studied in FYP 1. The project will involve integrating the essential components including the front\-end written in React, the back\-end created with Node.js, the database deployed with PostgreSQL, the object storage achieved with MinIO, the message queue using Redis, the AI microservice through the use of FastAPI, as well as the deployment done with Docker, into a working system.

Furthermore, the AI\-based tagging pipeline will be executed and incorporated into the asset uploading process. For this purpose, the AI models: CLIP, KeyBERT, YAMNet, and Whisper will be configured and tested using real assets of game development. Thus, the automatic generation of metadata of visual, text, and audio assets will be enabled so the efficiency of the suggested AI\-based metadata management approach is tested in practice.

Following the completion of the implementation phase, the process of testing will start to validate the proper functioning and reliability of the system. Functional testing will be conducted to confirm that every component works as intended. Special focus will be placed on areas such as authentication, workspaces, managing assets, generating metadata, search capabilities, tracking versions, collaboration, and admin features.

In addition, usability evaluation will involve independent game developers and potential users during the User Acceptance Testing process. This will allow us to obtain feedback from users regarding the effectiveness and usability of the platform. In order to obtain valid and measurable feedback, we may also utilize the System Usability Scale (SUS).

Eventually, in the future versions of the system, various potential improvements may be addressed. In particular, we may consider implementing more efficient AI technologies for metadata processing, integrating the system with other game creation instruments and engines, enabling the opportunity for real\-time editing, and developing mobile versions of the application as well.

## <a id="_Toc237556078"></a>Summary

This chapter presented the conclusion of the work completed during the Final Year Project 1 (FYP 1) phase. The achievements obtained throughout the project were discussed, including the knowledge gained in Digital Asset Management (DAM), Role\-Based Access Control (RBAC), artificial intelligence\-assisted metadata management, and modern web application development technologies. The project also successfully produced the required system analysis, literature review, system architecture, database design, UML diagrams, and interface prototypes for the proposed AI‑Enhanced Collaborative Digital Asset Management Platform.

